The esp32c3db BSP installs its chip definitions header to the wrong path, so
<bsp/irq.h> cannot be included from an application built against the installed
BSP.

bspesp32c3db.yml installs bsps/riscv/esp32/include/c3/chip_definitions.h to

    ${BSP_INCLUDEDIR}/${ESPRESSIF_CHIP_VARIANT}

and ESPRESSIF_CHIP_VARIANT is defined nowhere in the tree.  The only option of
that family is ESPRESSIF_CHIP_HEADER, in optvariant.yml, whose value is the
header path "c3/chip_definitions.h".  The undefined variable expands to nothing,
so the header lands in <bsp>/lib/include/chip_definitions.h.

Meanwhile bsp/irq.h does

    #include RTEMS_XSTRING( ESPRESSIF_CHIP_HEADER )

which resolves to "c3/chip_definitions.h" -- a path that does not exist in the
install tree.  Any application including <bsp/irq.h>, which is what an interrupt
handler needs, fails to compile:

    error: c3/chip_definitions.h: No such file or directory

In-tree builds of RTEMS' own testsuite are unaffected, because the source path
bsps/riscv/esp32/include/c3/chip_definitions.h resolves during those builds.
That is why it has gone unnoticed: it only breaks the case installing exists for.

Define the missing option rather than hardcoding "c3" in the destination, since
the author's use of a variable there clearly anticipated further variants.

Sent upstream; drop this when it lands.

diff --git a/spec/build/bsps/riscv/esp32/grp.yml b/spec/build/bsps/riscv/esp32/grp.yml
